WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.739 --> 00:00:05.356
Think about the last time you made a mistake.

2
00:00:05.356 --> 00:00:08.261
What did you say to yourself?

3
00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:12.452
Maybe it was, ‘I ruin everything,’ or ‘I should be better than this.’

4
00:00:12.452 --> 00:00:18.549
A harsh inner voice can turn one difficult moment into a judgment about your

5
00:00:18.549 --> 00:00:19.419
whole identity.

6
00:00:19.784 --> 00:00:22.999
Supportive self-talk can be more accurate—and more useful.

7
00:00:22.999 --> 00:00:27.018
Imagine you missed an important detail at work or school.

8
00:00:27.018 --> 00:00:29.831
‘I’m hopeless’ gives you very little direction.

9
00:00:29.831 --> 00:00:35.859
‘I missed that detail. I can correct it and make a reminder for next time’

10
00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:39.074
acknowledges what happened and leaves room for action.

11
00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:44.885
Try imagining what you would say to someone you care about in the same

12
00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:45.274
situation.

13
00:00:45.274 --> 00:00:50.608
Would you tell them one mistake defines them? Or would you help them

14
00:00:50.608 --> 00:00:54.300
understand what happened and what they could do next?

15
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.321
See whether one fair sentence could apply to you, too.

16
00:00:58.686 --> 00:01:03.233
You don’t have to believe a glowing statement about yourself. Start with

17
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:03.990
something believable.

18
00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.434
‘This is difficult.’

19
00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:06.764
‘I’m still learning.’

20
00:01:06.764 --> 00:01:09.686
‘I can take responsibility and ask for help.’

21
00:01:09.686 --> 00:01:14.764
A supportive inner voice makes room for honesty, repair, and another attempt.
